## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP with exactly k block selection, reconstruction, and non-adjacent constraint handling. Requires precomputing interval values (max-min) and a clean end-at-i transition that accounts for the gap requirement.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP with exactly k non-adjacent blocks
- Precomputation of val(l,r) = max(A[l..r]) - min(A[l..r]) using O(n²) sliding window enumeration
- Reconstruction pointers to retrieve endpoints
- Time complexity: O(n²) precomputation + O(kn²) DP; Space: O(n²) for val table + O(n·k) for DP
- Edge cases: k > ⌊n/2⌋ (impossible, return error), empty arrays (return 0)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSumIntervals(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const val = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let min = A[l], max = A[l];
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      min = Math.min(min, A[r]);
      max = Math.max(max, A[r]);
      val[l][r] = max - min;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const prev = j >= 2 ? dp[j-2][t-1] : (t === 1 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        if (prev === -Infinity) continue;
        const curr = prev + val[j-1][i-1];
        if (curr > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = curr;
          parent[i][t] = { start: j, end: i, prev: j - 2 };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t]) {
    const p = parent[i][t];
    if (!p.skip) {
      intervals.push([p.start, p.end]);
      i = p.prev;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], intervals: intervals.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores a new DP angle: subarray-level min as value, exact k non-overlapping blocks, with clear reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact block count
- Precomputation of subarray minima
- Backtracking to obtain endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Assumes A is 1-indexed, n = A.length-1, k given
function maxSumMinSubarrays(A, n, k) {
  const M = Array.from({length: n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for (let l=1; l<=n; l++) {
    let m = A[l];
    for (let r=l; r<=n; r++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[r]);
      M[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const opt = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
      for (let l=1; l<=i; l++) {
        const cand = dp[l-1][t-1] + M[l][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; opt[i][t] = l; }
      }
    }
  }
  // backtrack for endpoints
  let i = n, t = k, blocks = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = opt[i][t];
    blocks.push([l, i]);
    i = l-1; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a tree DP with multiple subtrees and an exact count constraint. Requires knapsack-style merging across children and careful reconstruction to report all k endpoints. Also checks edge-disjointness handling and correctness when k exceeds leaves.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with per-node knapsack merge
- Exact-path counting and edge-disjointness
- Result reconstruction: path endpoints
- Complexity reasoning: O(n k^2) time, O(n k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, k, val, children, dp) {
  let f = Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
  f[0] = 0;
  for (const v of children[u]) {
    let g = Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
      if (f[i] <= -Infinity) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t + i <= k; t++) {
        g[i+t] = Math.max(g[i+t], f[i] + dp[v][t]);
      }
    }
    f = g;
  }
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) dp[u][t] = f[t] + t * val[u];
}
```

